    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola given by the equation\n\n\\[\n\\frac{y^2}{27} - \\frac{x^2}{11} = 1,\n\\]\n\nwe start by identifying the standard form of a hyperbola. The equation is in the form\n\n\\[\n\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(a^2 = 27\\) and \\(b^2 = 11\\). For a hyperbola of this form, the vertices are located at \\((0, \\pm a)\\).\n\nFirst, we calculate \\(a\\):\n\n\\[\na = \\sqrt{27} = 3\\sqrt{3}.\n\\]\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola are at \\((0, \\pm 3\\sqrt{3})\\). The distance between the vertices is the distance between these two points, which is simply twice the value of \\(a\\):\n\n\\[\n2a = 2 \\times 3\\sqrt{3} = 6\\sqrt{3}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6\\sqrt{3}}.\n\\]",
