    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "The given equation of the hyperbola is \\(\\frac{y^2}{27} - \\frac{x^2}{11} = 1\\).\n\nTo determine the vertices of the hyperbola, we note the standard form of a hyperbola centered at the origin with a vertical transverse axis is \\(\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\\).\n\nFrom the equation, we identify \\(a^2 = 27\\) and \\(b^2 = 11\\). Therefore, \\(a = \\sqrt{27} = 3\\sqrt{3}\\) and \\(b = \\sqrt{11}\\).\n\nThe vertices of a hyperbola in this orientation are located at \\((0, \\pm a)\\). Hence, the vertices are at \\((0, 3\\sqrt{3})\\) and \\((0, -3\\sqrt{3})\\).\n\nThe distance between the vertices is the distance between these two points, which is the absolute difference in their \\(y\\)-coordinates. Thus, the distance \\(d\\) is:\n\\[d = |3\\sqrt{3} - (-3\\sqrt{3})| = |3\\sqrt{3} + 3\\sqrt{3}| = |6\\sqrt{3}| = 6\\sqrt{3}.\\]\n\nTherefore, the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola is \\(\\boxed{6\\sqrt{3}}\\).",
