      "text": "To find the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola given by the equation \\(\\frac{y^2}{27} - \\frac{x^2}{11} = 1\\), we need to identify the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) from the standard form of the hyperbola equation.\n\nThe standard form of a hyperbola that opens upwards and downwards is:\n\\[\n\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\n\\]\n\nBy comparing the given equation \\(\\frac{y^2}{27} - \\frac{x^2}{11} = 1\\) with the standard form, we can see that:\n\\[\na^2 = 27 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b^2 = 11\n\\]\n\nThe distance between the vertices of a hyperbola is given by \\(2a\\). Therefore, we need to find the value of \\(a\\).\n\nSince \\(a^2 = 27\\), we take the square root of both sides to find \\(a\\):\n\\[\na = \\sqrt{27} = 3\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nNow, the distance between the vertices is:\n\\[\n2a = 2 \\times 3\\sqrt{3} = 6\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]",
